+### Discard a failed request before session creation
+
+Only the dated epoch-2 bridge can discard a failed Pi request through a
+root-owned startup grant. There is no browser discard or recovery action.
+The grant names the exact request, receipt, preparation attempt and creation
+attempt. The two attempts can differ. A different request with the same name
+cannot authorize or resume the discard.
+
+This bridge only handles stopped, failed Pi new-session requests whose exact
+creating journal and unbound binding show that no native conversation was
+created. It also requires absence of any launch reservation, runtime authority,
+tracking, prior removal history, or lifecycle and resource ownership. The
+bridge refuses attachments and apparent attachment references in either saved
+creation goal. An unused draft ID is not an attachment claim, and the bridge
+does not change or release the draft. An unreadable or mismatched record is a
+reason to stop, not permission to discard it.
+
+Before changing any active records, the selected portal saves a private audit
+of their digests and the intended result and syncs the pending marker. The
+marker holds that name, the original retry, upload collection, and workspace
+package selection across interruption. Reading the original request status
+does not authorize recovery. Do not delete the marker or move the records by
+hand.
+
+The bridge keeps the original records privately and removes the pending marker
+only after the snapshot-free `cancelled` mapping and completion audit are
+durable. If removal succeeds but the portal cannot sync the marker's directory,
+the request has been discarded, but cleanup is unconfirmed. Other commands can
+admit work once the marker is absent. That does not prove cleanup, and replay
+of the original discard does not change a later owner of the name.
+
+Keep the bridge selected until it confirms cleanup for the original request.
+The portal checks the completed mapping and audit, syncs the marker's directory,
+and verifies that the marker is absent. If the original marker reappears after
+restart, only startup with the same verified grant can remove it, without
+retiring the request's records again. Starting the portal without a grant does
+not discard anything.
+
+If the portal cannot verify the grant, original owner or cleanup, it stops
+before serving. It does not retry creation or automatically restart to repeat
+the operation. Keep the bridge selected and reconcile only the original grant.
+The machine administrator removes that exact grant only after verifying its
+completed audit, terminal mapping and marker cleanup. A missing grant is not
+proof that the discard happened.
+
